SEC. 51P-913.109. URBAN DESIGN STANDARDS FOR MULTIFAMILY USES.

(a) Building facades more than 80 feet in length must incorporate at least three of the following design features or architectural elements:

(1) change in building materials, color, and texture that contrast with the rest of the building facade;

(2) articulation including projections, recesses, building material changes, parapets, cornices, or varying roof heights;

(3) distinguish between the building’s base (street level), middle (core mid- section), and top (upper level) using building material changes, architectural details, or changes in wall plane;

(4) deep inset windows;

(5) stepped-back floor plates for upper levels to diminish overall scale; or

(6) architectural elements that protrude from the face of the building, such as terraces, decks, balconies, overhangs, and sunshades.

(b) Each street-level street-facing dwelling unit must have direct access to the street from an improved path or walkway or a shared path or walkway between two units.

(c) A minimum of two of each of the following pedestrian amenities must be provided for every 300 linear feet of street frontage or fraction thereof:

(1) benches;

(2) trash receptacles; and

(3) bicycle racks (at least one of the two bicycle racks must hold a minimum five bicycles).

(d) For aboveground parking structures, solid screening of a minimum height of 42 inches, excluding driveways and entryways, measured from the floor level within the parking structure must be provided to screen vehicles.

(e) Openings in the parking structure facade may not exceed 55 percent of the total garage facade area. (Ord. 29334)
